# TLS for a station node. Caddy obtains and renews Let's Encrypt certificates
# on its own — there is no certbot sidecar and no renewal timer to forget.
#
# ONE name, one upstream:
#   EDGE_HOST → the connector's client edge, where a viber's paid pulls, the
#               x402 greeting, and the free GET /ilp self-description all land.
#
# There is no second hostname because NOTHING FREE IS SERVED FROM A STATION
# NODE. The relay bundle fronts a free-read WebSocket beside its paid edge;
# a station has no free surface to front — a viber browses the broadcaster
# page from the store and the hub's relay, never from here.
#
# CADDY DOES NOT CARRY THE RTMP PATH. Stock Caddy does not speak RTMP, and
# building a custom Caddy image would break the fleet's stock-TLS-front norm,
# so the origin publishes its own ingest port and terminates that TLS itself
# (TOON_INGEST_TLS_CERT / TOON_INGEST_TLS_KEY). Ingest appears nowhere in this
# file and cannot.
#
# The origin's SEGMENT port (3100) is deliberately absent from this file and
# from every route in it. It must never be publicly reachable: it is the
# payment-oblivious surface — segments, /now, /health and /encode all answer
# on it with no notion of a payment — and a public path to it is a free door
# onto a broadcaster's vibes.
#
# The upstream is NAMED, not addressed. Caddy re-resolves a container name per
# dial, so a recreate that moves `connector` to a new address on the compose
# network heals itself with no reload.

{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$EDGE_HOST} {
	reverse_proxy connector:3000
}
